<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bobo people</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bobo are a Mande-speaking people of western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burkina-faso">Burkina Faso</a> and neighbouring <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>, living mainly around <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bobo-dioulasso">Bobo-Dioulasso</a>, the city that bears their name. Farmers organised in largely autonomous villages, they are known for masks associated with Do, a spirit of renewal, used in initiation and funeral rites. In the 19th century many came under the Kingdom of Gwiriko, ruled from Sya.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bobo-people.jpg" alt="Bobo people" width="320" height="147" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Ferdinand Reus from Arnhem, Holland · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bobo%27s_along_the_river_the_Niger.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Bobo, sometimes called Bobo Fing, are a Mande people who live mainly in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burkina-faso">Burkina Faso</a>, with some in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mali">Mali</a>. They number approximately 110,000. The main Bobo community in the south is in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bobo-dioulasso">Bobo-Dioulasso</a>, the second-largest city of Burkina Faso and the old French colonial capital. Further north are large towns including Fô and Kouka, with Boura in the extreme north in Mali. In writings on African art the group around Bobo-Dioulasso is called Bobo-Fing, meaning black Bobo. The Bambara use the same name for another people, the Bobo-Oule or Bwa, who are a Gur people and not Mande.</p><p>The Bobo are descended from an ancient aggregation that assembled around a number of core clans, none of which preserves oral traditions of migration into the area. Their language and culture are closer to those of their Mande neighbours, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bambara-people">Bamana</a> and the Minianka, than to those of the Gurunsi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mossi">Mossi</a>, and they are best seen as a southern extension of the Mande rather than a recent intrusion. Although over 41 per cent of Bobo lineages claim a foreign origin, they also say they are autochthonous.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Farming is central to Bobo life. The main food crops are red <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sorghum">sorghum</a>, pearl <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/millet">millet</a>, yams and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maize">maize</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cotton">cotton</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peanut">peanuts</a> are the chief cash crops. The cotton is sold to textile mills in Koudougou. Colonial rule and the building of these mills broke down the local co-operative labour systems that had bound Bobo society together.</p><p>The lineage is the basic unit. Each village is organised by the relations among patrilines, and a lineage unites the descendants of a common ancestor, called the wakoma. Political power in the hands of one person is foreign to the Bobo, who are ruled by groups of elders deciding together. Each clan has a totem, and a Bobo introduces himself with a given name, a clan name and the totem he respects. Polygyny is common, and levirate and sororate marriages are practised.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>In Bobo belief the creator god Wuro formed the earth from a ball of clay and ordered creation into opposing pairs, such as male and female, village and bush, culture and nature. Wuro gave the first mask to the first man, a blacksmith, and the Bobo still make masks in his honour. Blacksmiths traditionally worship Wuro's son Dwo. The masks have wooden structures rising above the main body and are painted red, black and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> or white, repainted at the start of each season. They honour Wuro and are held to erase evil and balance the sun, earth and rain. Some Bobo have converted to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christianity">Christianity</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/islam">Islam</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bobo people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>He established his rule over the <b>Bobo</b>, Bwa and other farming peoples, with Sya, later Bobo-Dioulasso, as the main centre.</blockquote><a href="/state/kingdom-of-gwiriko">Kingdom of Gwiriko</a></li></ul></section>
